// A dictionary written where it is (GLB-132, 2026-10-16) — and every other
// name that holds it keeps what it held.
//
// The three engines find a key by hash now, and write into a dictionary in
// place when its variable is its only holder. That is safe only if a second
// holder — another name, an argument, a value taken in a loop — makes the
// next write copy. Each line below has one.
d = #(a: 1)
d["b"]$~ 2
e = d
d["c"]$~ 3
d["a"]$~ 9
>> d " " e ¶
f = e
e["b"]$~ 7
e["z"]$~ 0
>> d " " e " " f ¶
g(x) {
    x["q"]$~ 1
    <~ x
}
h = g(d)
>> d " " h ¶
m = #()
n = #()
@ i:1..5 {
    k = "k" i
    m[k]$~ i
    ? i == 3 { n = m }
}
>> m " " n ¶
m$-["k2"]
>> m " " m["k4"] ¶
!? { >> m["k2"] ¶ } :! { >> _err ¶ }
>> (m == #(k5: 5, k1: 1, k3: 3, k4: 4)) ¶
